.5629668016335927e-05,-0.00025674383505247533],"request":{"active_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,785,738,315,3501,4930,87,7358,22669,15087,429,26553,198,78045,17,87,284,220,18,88,284,481,89,59,60,285,264,1555,382,785,738,315,3501,4930,87,7358,22669,15087,429,26553,198,78045,21,87,284,481,88,284,481,19,89,59,60,285,2441,1555,382,9885,279,9210,1948,1493,5128,11,304,12348,13,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nThe set of points $(x,y,z)$ that satisfy\n\\[2x = 3y = -z\\]is a line.\n\nThe set of points $(x,y,z)$ that satisfy\n\\[6x = -y = -4z\\]is another line.\n\nFind the angle between these lines, in degrees.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"f045b09de53842fd533792ede3e908086bf157ae91bc9b45e66d907607a07bd7"},"seconds":36.504289388656616,"seed":663259945619055643,"stop":"eos","text":"To find the angle between the two lines defined by the point sets given, we first need to find direction vectors for each line.\n\nStarting with the first set of equations:\n\\[2x = 3y = -z\\]\n\nLet \\(2x = 3y = -z = k\\), for some constant \\(k\\). Then we have:\n\\[x = \\frac{k}{2}, \\quad y = \\frac{k}{3}, \\quad z = -k\\]\n\nThus, the direction vector for this line is:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{v}_1 = \\left( \\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{1}{3}, -1 \\right)\n\\]\n\nNext, we consider the second set of equations:\n\\[6x = -y = -4z\\]\n\nLet \\(6x = -y = -4z = m\\), for some constant \\(m\\). Then we have:\n\\[x = \\frac{m}{6}, \\quad y = -m, \\quad z = -\\frac{m}{4}\\]\n\nThus, the direction vector for this line is:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\left( \\frac{1}{6}, -1, -\\frac{1}{4} \\right)\n\\]\n\nTo find the angle \\(\\theta\\) between these two lines, we use the dot product formula:\n\\[\n\\cos \\theta = \\frac{\\mathbf{v}_1 \\cdot \\mathbf{v}_2}{\\|\\mathbf{v}_1\\| \\|\\mathbf{v}_2\\|}\n\\]\n\nFirst, we compute the dot product \\(\\mathbf{v}_1 \\cdot \\mathbf{v}_2\\):\n\\[\n\\mathbf{v}_1 \\cdot \\mathbf{v}_2 = \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) \\left( \\frac{1}{6} \\right) + \\left( \\frac{1}{3} \\right) (-1) + (-1) \\left( -\\frac{1}{4} \\right)\n\\]\n\\[\n= \\frac{1}{12} - \\frac{1}{3} + \\frac{1}{4}\n\\]\n\nConvert all terms to a common denominator (12):\n\\[\n= \\frac{1}{12} - \\frac{4}{12} + \\frac{3}{12}\n\\]\n\\[\n= \\frac{1 - 4 + 3}{12} = \\frac{0}{12} = 0\n\\]\n\nSince the dot product is zero, the vectors are orthogonal, and the angle between them is \\(90^\\circ\\).\n\nThus, the angle between the lines is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{90}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{90}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
